  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 855 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nDeep and slow moving upper level low pressure will continue to \nslowly drift eastward over the Treasure State over the next 36-48 \nhours, bringing a prolonged period of cool and wet weather. \nOvernight showers and thunderstorms that moved through the area \nwill gradually coalesce across North Central Montana and along the\nRocky Mountain Front, where they will more or less persist \nthrough the day today and into early Tuesday. \n\nA brief period of respite from the wet weather will arrive \nWednesday and Thursday as weak upper level ridging transits the \narea. As the ridge moves overhead, temperatures will be a bit \ncloser to average, with highs generally in the 70s to low 80s \nacross the lower elevations. This warmer and drier period will not\nlast long, however, as our next upper level low will push in \nFriday and Saturday, bringing another prolonged period of cooler \nand wetter weather. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nRain through Tuesday: Models remain in overall good agreement \nthat the heaviest rainfall will generally remain confined to the \nRocky Mountain Front and along the Hi-Line, where amounts of 1.5 \nto 3 inches of additional rainfall remain possible. Areas that \nreceived heavy rainfall from the strong to severe thunderstorms \nthat rolled through the area Sunday night will need to be watched \nextra closely, as this extra moisture may provide the antecedent \nconditions needed for the rainfall to over saturate some of the \nsoils locally and result in some localized flooding. Ludwig",
